 **Proof technique:** identify the dipole coefficient by differentiating its source moment and integrating continuity by parts, apply vector-valued Taylor's theorem pointwise in the source position, then propagate the absolute remainder through the transverse field and power formulas.
 
-1.1 Dipole moment identities. For each $i\in\{1,2,3\}$ put $p_i(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho(y,u)\,dy$. Smoothness and vanishing of $\rho,J$ for $|y|>a$ imply that $\rho$, $J_i$ and their time derivatives vanish on $\partial B_a$. Thus the integrals of $y_i\rho$ and $y_i\rho_t$ over $B_a$ agree with those over the compact measurable finite-measure set $\overline B_a$ [F7]. Apply the set form of [F3] with integration set $\overline B_a$ and parameter $u$ to $f_i(y,u)=y_i\rho(y,u)$; its parameter derivative is $y_i\rho_t(y,u)$, and both are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. This gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho_t(y,u)\,dy$. Fix $R_0>a$ and apply the divergence theorem to $y\mapsto y_iJ(y,u)$ on $B_{R_0}$. The boundary flux is zero because $J=0$ near $\partial B_{R_0}$, while $\operatorname{div}(y_iJ)=J_i+y_i\operatorname{div}J$. Using $\rho_t=-\operatorname{div}J$ and the vanishing of the source outside $B_a$ gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}J_i(y,u)\,dy$. For each $i$, the integrals of $J_i$ and $J_{i,t}$ over $B_a$ agree with those over $\overline B_a$; the closed ball is compact measurable of finite measure by [F7]. Both integrands are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. Apply the set form of [F3] on $\overline B_a$ separately to each component $J_i$ with parameter $u$; differentiating the preceding identity gives $p_i''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_{i,t}(y,u)\,dy$. Collecting the three component identities yields $p''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_t(y,u)\,dy$. [given, F1, F2, F3, F7, algebra, A1]
+1.1 Dipole moment identities. The compact-ball verification in Proof step 1.2 of the preceding transverse-radiation theorem [F1] applies to the same $a>0$ and Countable Choice hypothesis: $\overline B_a$ is closed and bounded, hence compact; it is Borel and Lebesgue measurable; and $\lambda_3(\overline B_a)\le\lambda_3(B_{2a})<\infty$. Thus it is an admissible fixed integration set for [F3]. For each $i\in\{1,2,3\}$ put $p_i(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho(y,u)\,dy$. Smoothness and vanishing of $\rho,J$ for $|y|>a$ imply that $\rho$, $J_i$ and their time derivatives vanish on $\partial B_a$. Thus the integrals of $y_i\rho$ and $y_i\rho_t$ over $B_a$ agree with those over that compact measurable finite-measure set $\overline B_a$. Apply the set form of [F3] with integration set $\overline B_a$ and parameter $u$ to $f_i(y,u)=y_i\rho(y,u)$; its parameter derivative is $y_i\rho_t(y,u)$, and both are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. This gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho_t(y,u)\,dy$. Fix $R_0>a$ and apply the divergence theorem to $y\mapsto y_iJ(y,u)$ on $B_{R_0}$. The boundary flux is zero because $J=0$ near $\partial B_{R_0}$, while $\operatorname{div}(y_iJ)=J_i+y_i\operatorname{div}J$. Using $\rho_t=-\operatorname{div}J$ and the vanishing of the source outside $B_a$ gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}J_i(y,u)\,dy$. For each $i$, the integrals of $J_i$ and $J_{i,t}$ over $B_a$ agree with those over $\overline B_a$; the closed ball has the compactness, measurability and finite measure just verified using [F1]. Both integrands are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. Apply the set form of [F3] on $\overline B_a$ separately to each component $J_i$ with parameter $u$; differentiating the preceding identity gives $p_i''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_{i,t}(y,u)\,dy$. Collecting the three component identities yields $p''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_t(y,u)\,dy$. [given, F1, F2, F3, algebra, A1]
 
 1.2 Finiteness of the remainder. For fixed $u$, define $g_u(y):=\max_{|s-u|\le a/c}|\partial_t^{N+2}J(y,s)|$. The maximum exists and is finite by [F8]. For $y,y'\in\overline B_a$, $\displaystyle |g_u(y)-g_u(y')|\le\sup_{|s-u|\le a/c}\left||\partial_t^{N+2}J(y,s)|-|\partial_t^{N+2}J(y',s)|\right|\le\sup_{|s-u|\le a/c}|\partial_t^{N+2}J(y,s)-\partial_t^{N+2}J(y',s)|.$ Uniform continuity in [F8] makes the last expression tend to zero as $y'\to y$. Thus $g_u$ is continuous and bounded on $\overline B_a$, hence integrable on $B_a$; this proves that $\mathcal R_N(u)$ is finite. [given, F6, F8, A1]
 
